retroarch (1.7.3-1) bionic; urgency=low

  * This version includes an upstream change to the default value for
    'input_driver' (exposed in Settings 🢡 Driver 🢡 Input Driver in the GUI)
    from 'udev' to 'x'. If you are updating RetroArch from a previous
    installation, this may cause your keyboard to be suddenly unresponsive.
    Resolving the issue typically entails either adding your user account to the
    'input' group, a la…
        $ usermod -a -G input <username>
    …or manually changing the value for the 'input_driver' key in retroarch.cfg
    (located in "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/retroarch", by default) from
    'udev' to 'x'.

